BEYOND HIDING IN PLAIN SIGHT  |  THEORY-BASED DESIGN PROPOSAL
BEYOND HIDING
IN PLAIN SIGHT
A Theory-Based Proposal for Authenticated,
Image-Carried Cryptographic Messaging
Architecture • Key Management • Steganography • Distributed Access Control
Design evolution, threat model, and evaluation programme
Consolidated research thesis and design rationale
Version 1.0 • 25 October 2026
STATUS: PROPOSED THEORY AND ARCHITECTURE
This document describes a proposed system and a research programme. It does not claim that the complete protocol has been implemented, formally verified, independently audited, or proven secure.

Abstract
This thesis proposes an architecture for secure first-contact messaging in which encrypted messages may be carried by images and, where useful, concealed through steganography. The design goal is not to invent a new cipher. It is to combine established cryptographic mechanisms with a carefully bounded relay, explicit identity verification, optional image-based concealment, linked message capsules, and clearly defined access and recovery policies.
The proposal separates properties that are often mistakenly conflated. Authenticated encryption protects message confidentiality and detects tampering; authenticated key agreement and explicit identity checks address who is communicating with whom; steganography attempts to make the existence of a payload less apparent; capsule manifests make multi-part messages parseable and verifiable; and threshold secret sharing, if adopted, can require multiple independent shares to recover a secret. None of these functions should be treated as a substitute for another.
A key contribution of the work is its record of design evolution. Ideas such as deriving secret material from a unique or remote image, using generic double encryption to achieve both secrecy and identity, and treating a chain of image references as a distributed secret are examined against their underlying assumptions. The revised proposal treats image carriers as transport or concealment objects rather than sources of cryptographic entropy, replaces vague encryption layering with distinct security properties, and separates ciphertext fragmentation from threshold-based secret control.
The thesis presents a threat model, candidate protocol components, lifecycle limits, failure cases, and an evaluation programme. Its conclusions are deliberately bounded: the architecture is a theory-based proposal requiring precise protocol specification, adversarial testing, cryptographic review, usability evaluation, and operational hardening before deployment with sensitive information.
Executive Summary
• Core proposition: build an authenticated messaging protocol first, then optionally carry its encrypted payload in an image.
• Trust boundary: endpoints hold identity and session secrets; the bot, transport, image provider, and storage services are untrusted with respect to plaintext confidentiality.
• First contact: public-key agreement alone does not prove human identity. Users need an explicit key-verification mechanism, such as a safety code or independently verified fingerprint.
• Cryptography: select maintained implementations of established primitives. Candidate components include X25519-based authenticated key agreement, HKDF-SHA-256, Ed25519 signatures where appropriate, and XChaCha20-Poly1305 AEAD. The exact protocol and library choices remain subject to review.
• Images: image uniqueness, URLs, metadata, and hashes are not secret entropy. A remote image service must not be an indispensable decryption dependency.
• Multi-image messages: bind fragments to a versioned manifest and reject missing, duplicated, reordered, or mixed fragments. This is not the same as splitting a decryption secret.
• Access control: threshold schemes are optional and should be introduced only for a defined use case. Expiry and revocation can restrict future actions but cannot recall plaintext already copied.
• Evidence status: this is a design proposal, not an audited protocol or proof of security.
How to Read This Thesis
The document distinguishes three categories of statement. “Established principle” refers to a widely accepted cryptographic or systems concept. “Proposed design” describes the architecture recommended here. “Open question” marks an issue that needs a concrete decision, testing, or independent review. This distinction matters: naming a sound primitive does not automatically make a protocol sound.
The original product and cryptography specifications are treated as the project’s baseline vocabulary and architecture. This thesis consolidates those specifications with the preceding exploration of salts, nonces, image-derived material, double encryption, linked capsules, and remote revocation. Where the earlier exploratory idea is not documented as an agreed requirement, it is presented as a design question rather than a historical fact.
